theory.intro:
A closed cylinder has three faces: two circular ends and one curved side. Unrolled, the curved side is a rectangle of length $2\pi r$ and width $h$. Adding the faces gives $A = 2\pi r^{2} + 2\pi rh$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.55]
\draw (-3,0)--(-3,8);
\draw (3,0)--(3,8) node[midway,right] {$h$};
\draw (0,8) ellipse (3 and 0.9);
\draw (-3,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=360,x radius=3,y radius=0.9];
\draw[dashed] (-3,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=0,x radius=3,y radius=0.9];
\fill (0,8) circle (2pt);
\draw (0,8)--(3,8) node[midway,above,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$r$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
$2\pi r^{2}$ is the **two circular ends**, each of area $\pi r^{2}$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.7]
\draw (0,0) circle (2.2);
\node at (0,0) {$\pi r^{2}$};
\draw (5.4,0) circle (2.2);
\node at (5.4,0) {$\pi r^{2}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$2\pi rh$ is the **curved surface**, which unrolls into a rectangle of length $2\pi r$ and width $h$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.7]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (7,3);
\node at (3.5,1.5) {curved surface};
\node[below] at (3.5,0) {$2\pi r$};
\node[right] at (7,1.5) {$h$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

